Subject: [PATCH] soc: qcom: ocmem: Fix PSGSC control register stride

The PSGSC control registers are 32-bit and four bytes apart. Google's
GPL angler 3.10 driver has PSCGC_CTL_IDX(x) = (x) * 0x4, but mainline
used a one-byte stride. On a SoC with more than one OCMEM region, the
second and later ocmem_write() calls therefore hit unaligned
addresses.

On msm8994 (three regions) the GPU's first ocmem_allocate() took an
alignment fault in update_range() and the probe oopsed before the
Adreno could be initialized.

Use the four-byte stride. Evidence: android-msm-angler-3.10-oreo-mr1,
drivers/soc/qcom/ocmem_core.c, PSCGC_CTL_IDX() and PSCGC_CTL_n().

diff --git a/drivers/soc/qcom/ocmem.c b/drivers/soc/qcom/ocmem.c
--- a/drivers/soc/qcom/ocmem.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/qcom/ocmem.c
@@ -92,7 +92,7 @@
 #define OCMEM_REG_GEN_STATUS			0x0000000c
 
 #define OCMEM_REG_PSGSC_STATUS			0x00000038
-#define OCMEM_REG_PSGSC_CTL(i0)			(0x0000003c + 0x1*(i0))
+#define OCMEM_REG_PSGSC_CTL(i0)			(0x0000003c + 0x4 * (i0))
 
 #define OCMEM_PSGSC_CTL_MACRO0_MODE(val)	FIELD_PREP(0x00000007, (val))
 #define OCMEM_PSGSC_CTL_MACRO1_MODE(val)	FIELD_PREP(0x00000070, (val))
